public class Calculator {
    void calculate(int a, int b) {
        System.out.println("Sum (int): " + (a + b));
    }
    void calculate(double a, double b) {
        System.out.println("Sum (double): " + (a + b));
    }
    void calculate(int a, int b, int c) {
        System.out.println("Sum (3 ints): " + (a + b + c));
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Calculator calc = new Calculator();
        calc.calculate(5, 10);           // Calls first method
        calc.calculate(5.5, 6.3);        // Calls second method
        calc.calculate(1, 2, 3);         // Calls third method
    }
}
JVM Method Resolution:
The Java compiler determines which calculate() method to invoke at compile time, based on the number and type of arguments.
This is why it's called compile-time polymorphism.
Benefits:
Code clarity: A single method name can perform logically similar operations without confusion.
Ease of use: Users don�t need to remember multiple method names.
Flexibility: The same method can handle various input types or conditions.
